Date: Thu, 11 Feb 93 16:07:22 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: Economic benefits of liberty

   From: Mark Read Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>

   In the real world, "dramatic increases in personal freedom" mean dramatic
   increases in cash income, not "small loss."  Why not emphasize economic
   benefits?  The "swamp" exists only in the minds of people who do not yet
   understand economics.

I would hazard that nobody understands economics well enough to be
assured that the complete elimination of government will cause
dramatic increases in "cash income".  Economics is hardly physics, you
know.

Now, it may just be possible to show that the elimination of
government will cause an increase in the overall GDP.  But even that
would ignore the possibly large changes in the *distribution*.  And
many people care a lot about the distribution, especially people whose
share may decrease.  God, just ready any leftist propaganda, and you
will see more references to "fairness" than you can stomach!  The
Boston Globe had a long article on a new bill in the Mass. legislature
to increase school funding.  But there were many more references to
the fact that it would increase "equity" (i.e., even out per-pupil
spending) than that it would increase "quality" (i.e., increase
per-pupil spending).

Even worse is the tendency of many people to be far more interested in
security than overall benefits.  The Boston Globe (a great source of
leftist examples!) today had an article on workers in the "underground
economy", nothing that they were "exposed to the vicissitudes of the
job market" and would much prefer a "secure job".

Ugh.

Dale

