   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   In general, the absense of a clear incentive to form large communities, 
   coupled with the problems that such communities would face, the problems 
   associated with starting draconian community regulations, [...]

Perhaps the lack of large privately-regulated communities is because
of the existence of governmentally-based communities that can take
care of needs on the larger geographic scales?  Much like education,
the presence of a governmental service tends to prevent the
development of a similar private service.

As early as the 1700's, there were perceived needs on scales of 1000
miles that the existing governments (on scales of 100 miles) did not
satisfy.  Hence, the United States was formed.

These days, there is a tendency to try to satisfy the need of "peace
over the world" (on a scale of 10,000 miles), for which there is no
mechanism at all, so the UN is starting to be cast into that role.

That's not to argue that governments are necessarily the best way to
satisfy these needs, but it seems that there is evidence that needs
that inherently involve large geographic scales exist.

Dale

