From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON: Quick bucks

   From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu

   > A lot of buyers of residential real estate got caught in the same
   > bind.  As I was told in 1987, "Invest in real estate!  It can't
   > possibly go down!"  Yeah, right.

   I'm curious. Do you think the Tax Reform Act of 1986 had anything
   to do with this?

I've read things which suggested that investing in real-estate became
less favorable after the Tax Reform Act.  But since I wasn't
interested anyway, I didn't look into it.  As far as I know, the bulk
of the favorable tax treatment of residental homeownership has
remained, though.

One thing I *did* learn was how to detect when a "speculative bubble"
is about to burst.  I've seen three of them now (Midwestern farm land
in 1979, stocks in 1987, Mass. real estate in 1989), and the symptoms are
always exactly the same: (1) brokers argue that traditional measures
of the value of the asset are irrelevant, and that the price can rise
without limit, (2) lemmings start buying because "you can't loose
money", and (3) people start borrowing money to buy more, and lenders
are willing to lend as much as people are willing to borrow.

Dale

