Date: Thu, 11 Feb 93 15:23:30 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Quick bucks

Dale Worley writes:
>One thing I *did* learn was how to detect when a "speculative bubble"
>is about to burst.  I've seen three of them now (Midwestern farm land
>in 1979, stocks in 1987, Mass. real estate in 1989), and the symptoms are
>always exactly the same: ...

Speculative bubbles are indeed a real economic problem, and the best
cure seems to be to give people experience with them.  Experimental
markets also have bubbles which go away after they players have been
in a few of them.

High School kids or younger should have kids play such games,
providing the social benefit of supressing bubbles and the private
benefit of helping these kids avoid bad investments later.  If only
someone would hire me to teach econ to high school kids :-).  There
are many other such games which can teach valuable lessons, and don't
require deep math or analysis to understand.

Robin Hanson

