>
>Yeah, what Romana said.  I've been meaning to ask folks:  Isn't
>makeup, plastic surgery, etc. just the sort of transhuman
>self-transformation that should the peak of extropian correctness?
>Surely you didn't expect folks to stick implants in their brains while
>keeping that big nose and scweaky voice they've always hated.  

Yeah, what Robin said.  

I know people who feel that some level of voluntary body modification is
okay:

  1. Hair styling, shaving, plucking, makeup, tattooing, piercing,
     reconstructive surgery after disease or accident.

However, some of those same people feel that artificially changing
abilities, lifespan, apparent age, race, or sex might be somehow less
ethical--maybe even disrespectful to our creator(s):

  2. Memory and computation enhancement brain implants, artificial sense
     organs, uploading, organ transplants from cloned tissue, face lifts
     and modifications, breast enhancement/reduction, derm abrasion and
     dying, sex reassignment, morphing via nanotech, etc. (betchya never
     put those things in one "category" before :^)

Somehow, that second category of transformation invades a sacred space--
like the body shouldn't be messed with TOO much.  Don't cheat!  Don't use
invasive technology to make you think better, or change your perceptions,
or live longer, or appear younger, or of a different bloodline or sex (or
even species!) That might offend the god[desse]s who created you with a
specific design in mind.  Bonus worry for those who believe in
reincarnation: If you don't learn your life lessons this time in the
intended manner, by trudging through and coping with your original
equipment and perspective, then you might be sent through in the same form
again, and AGAIN, until you finally accept your fate, and teach others how
to accept their similar fates.  Ack!

Is it more virtuous to fake a youthful appearance very temporarily (with
makeup, for instance) vs. less temporarily with surgery?  Is it
disrespectful to mimic other individuals or races in appearance with
surgery and dyes?

Is it ethically better to fix our bodies because of accident or disease
vs. "fixing" them to please our own new designs?

Assuming self-motivation and full consentuality, why should it be
ethically different to make one kind of body modification vs. another?  Is
there really a sacred line we should avoid crossing, lest we loose a
valuable part of our individual self, or [goddess forbid] our humanity?

At what ratio is a wetware/hardware hybrid no longer human?  Does a
less-than-100% stock equipment human deserve less respect or love?  (Is
that even a meaningful question since the cells in a "stock" human are all
replaced so many times, anyway?)  Are cyborgs inherently less valuable
than "pure" humans?  Will machines steal our souls once we become truly
symbiotic (by our own design, no less)?  Does it matter?

I'm sure you can tell from my tone that I fully support voluntary
self-transformation of appearance or composition.  I guess I wrote this to
see if any of you have a different perspective--am I overlooking anything
besides tradition, prejudice, fear, and general social disruption as
reasons not to upgrade our bodies and minds?

